data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` with `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** The test confirmed both work without issues:

```csharp
Span<nint> nints = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> nuints = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

This has been supported since C# 9 / .NET 5, when `nint`/`nuint` were introduced as first-class types. Since they're unmanaged types (8 bytes on 64-bit, 4 bytes on 32-bit), they satisfy the `stackalloc` constraint just like `int` or `long`.
